<p>PayPal co-founder Peter Thiel has spoken publicly on a variety of issues from contrarian entrepreneurship to technical stagnation to geopolitical struggle. Lately, he’s drawn more attention with extended remarks on what would seem an unexpected subject: the Antichrist, a seemingly enlightened but deceptively malevolent figure or movement bent on putting the world under&nbsp;innovation-stifling unified control. The term&nbsp;itself may sound familiar, though many of us haven’t seen it referenced much in popular culture since the heyday of the <em>Left Behind</em> novel series that sold so well around the turn of the millennium. Whether or not it’s to your literary taste, that apocalypse narrative’s villain does adhere to the Antichrist archetype laid out in the Bible.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4cG50jVy-3M">the new <em>Hochelaga</em> video above</a>, Tommie Trelawny goes back to the biblical texts to determine the origins of the Antichrist, whom humanity has portrayed as “a great deceiver who charms the world through false miracles, and with it, demands to be worshiped as god himself.” However large he looms in modern Christian storytelling, he’s referenced in only four passages of the Bible. The word <em>Antichrist</em> appears nowhere but in one of John’s epistles, which also warns that “even now many antichrists have come,” indicating, as Trelawny puts it, those who reject “that Jesus is the Christ, or Messiah.” As John saw it, some such individuals — including members of new sects like <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2025/12/the-gnostic-gospels-an-introduction-to-the-forbidden-teachings-of-jesus.html">Gnosticism</a> — were also dangerously charismatic, which aided the spread of their false doctrines.</p>
