<p>The apostle Paul or one of his disciples, in a letter to the Thessalonians, foresees the rise of “a mysterious figure known only as ‘the lawless one,’ who will gain power by deceiving people through false miracles, and once in power, he will commit his greatest crime of all: ‘He will oppose and exalt himself over everything that is called God or worshiped, so that he sets himself up in God’s temple, proclaiming himself to be God.” For its part, the Book of Revelation adds “the arrival of a monstrous seven-headed beast,” and later, a companion with “the horns of a lamb but the voice of a dragon.” Thereafter comes the “mark of the beast,” 666 — or perhaps 616, Trelawny adds, given that either could be seen as code for <em>Neron Kaisar</em> in Greek or <em>Nero Caesar&nbsp;</em>in Latin, better known as the ancient <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nero">Roman Emperor Nero</a>. Some suspected him of being the Antichrist, especially if he orchestrated Rome’s great fire in order to consolidate his power and scapegoat the Christians.</p>
<p>Trelawny also gets into detail about the twice-excommunicated Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II, whom Pope Gregory IX saw as the prophesied “lawless one,” and even into the accusations of being the Antichrist flung back and forth during the Protestant Reformation and the flurry of printed pamphlets that drove its surrounding controversy. The latter story “shows a subtle change taking place in how people could understand the Antichrist. It was no longer just a single person; it could be an entire system or institution.” Throughout history, as underscored today by anonymous online debates as well as <a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/peter-thiel-brings-his-lectures-on-the-antichrist-to-the-vaticans-doorstep">lectures delivered by Peter Thiel in Rome</a>, “it seems that whatever people fear the most, from communism to globalism to regulation, the Antichrist can become.” If so, we’ll keep hearing about his imminent (or already accomplished) rise until humanity can get over its fears — and what a miracle that would be.</p>
